#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/e5a5837a105098f1a551eb7f338017a2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$x = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0)$.\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n$x=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6)$." :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n$x= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6)$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$x=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "The quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/f94daa181a489d90232901e1c3c781aa.png)\n\n$x = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$x = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6$. So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are\n$x=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/a6dac4cb26046fd6ff579751230819f8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to\n\n$$\n\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{x - 2}{3} & = \\cos   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{9} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{y + 4}{3} & = \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{9} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta & = 1 \\\\ \\frac{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}{9} + \\frac{( y + 4 {)}^{2}}{9} & = 1 \\\\ ( x - 2 {)}^{2} + ( y + 4 {)}^{2} & = 9.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c4f1d7-805b-5a9e-adc4-f99d48079b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086767} {:answer/id #uuid "b517eaf0-d448-5440-9933-4a4e5231d3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086768} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ec48c-a48b-582b-98e0-0c451cfb7b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086769} {:answer/id #uuid "f17614e4-8955-5095-98f0-7976720ae2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\pi , \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086770} {:answer/id #uuid "347b457d-4b2d-5b60-85c9-6e93bd53eec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c4f1d7-805b-5a9e-adc4-f99d48079b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086767} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69c06b0-5b85-58f8-bf67-4a7baa78004e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086766}] :question/id #uuid "f9ceb55c-d1e9-5689-8032-0bd11852d402" :question/math-academy-id "q-78681" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/806/Source/Images/q-78681.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 7\\cos \\theta , y = 7\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?"}]}
